<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Ferrara</h1><p class="st-meta">1264 CE – 1598 CE · capital Ferrara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Este state on the Po whose court made Ferrara a capital of Renaissance poetry, painting and town planning.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1264 the citizens of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-ferrara">Commune of Ferrara</a> proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obizzo-ii-deste">Obizzo II d'Este</a> their perpetual lord. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">Este</a>, a feudal family with lands near <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-padua">Padua</a>, held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> as papal vicars and added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reggio-emilia">Reggio</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-ii">Pope Paul II</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borso-deste">Borso d'Este</a> duke of Ferrara in 1471.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">Este</a> ran a brilliant court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ercole-i-d-este">Ercole I</a>'s planned extension of the city, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addizione-erculea">Addizione Erculea</a>, was one of the first modern urban plans. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> supported the poets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matteo-maria-boiardo">Boiardo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-ariosto">Ariosto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torquato-tasso">Tasso</a> and the painters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palazzo-schifanoia">Schifanoia</a> frescoes. Alfonso I, husband of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucrezia-borgia">Lucrezia Borgia</a>, was famous for his artillery. The duchy was a papal fief, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> was held from the emperor.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ii-deste">Alfonso II</a> died without a legitimate heir in 1597. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-viii">Pope Clement VIII</a> refused to recognise his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-d-este-duke-of-modena">Cesare</a>, and in 1598 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">Este</a> moved their court to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-modena-and-reggio">Duchy of Modena and Reggio</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-ferrara.jpg" alt="Duchy of Ferrara" width="320" height="235"><figcaption>Image: Durero · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ducado_de_Modena_(antes_de_1830).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1264 CE – 1598 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ferrara</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Borso d'Este, Ercole I d'Este, Alfonso I d'Este</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Emilian, Italian, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Este Ferrara, Lordship of Ferrara</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1431&amp;focus=duchy-of-ferrara" aria-label="Duchy of Ferrara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4957 593 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Ferrara lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5277 849L5304 942M5277 849L5261 855M5277 849L5277 849M5277 849L5261 855M5277 849L5254 854"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5261 855h0M5277 849h0M5261 855h0M5254 854h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="